COMM Magnetometer System
COMM ----------------------------------------------------------------------
COMM Geometrics G822A   Caesium Vapour magnetometer sensor, mounted on the 
COMM                    front of the Tx loop frame.
COMM Kroum VS KMAG4     Counter, sample interval 50Hz, (Down sampled in    
COMM                    processing)
COMM Base Magnetometer  GEM Systems GSM19 proton precession sample at 1 sec
COMM
COMM ----------------------------------------------------------------------
COMM DATA PROCESSING
COMM ----------------------------------------------------------------------
COMM
COMM GPS POSITION
COMM Only the Omnistar HP differentially corrected GPS, GP2 position       
COMM information were used for the survey. The data were recorded  
COMM in the WGS84 datum.                                                   
COMM The GPS positions were then translated to the centre of the frame     
COMM based on the instrument x, y and z positions on the system frame.     
COMM The corrected positions were transformed to GDA2020 datum, Map Grid of  
COMM Australia Zone 50 Projection
COMM 
COMM LASER ALTIMETER
COMM The height processing involves manual and automated routines using a  
COMM combination of the SkyTEM in-house software and comercial packages.   
COMM The processing involves the following steps:                          
COMM Keeping the 5 largest of the 30 values acquired per second, and       
COMM discarding the remainder to correct for the canopy effect;            
COMM 3 sec running box-car filter (smoothing filter);                      
COMM Tilt correction, using the inclinometer data, to account for the      
COMM altimeter not pointing vertically downward;                           
COMM Averaging of the tilt corrected values from the two laser altimeters; 
COMM A 3 sec low pass filter is then applied to the final result.          
COMM 
COMM ELECTROMAGNETIC DATA 
COMM Raw (binary) SkyTEM data have been processed using SkyTEM proprietary 
COMM software.
COMM Prior to processing, primary field correction (PFC) was applied to    
COMM the early LM moment gates (9 to 14) to remove the effects of residual 
COMM currents that occur due to magnetic coupling between the receiver     
COMM coils and the transmitter loop. PFC is performed by collecting        
COMM Low Moment data whilst flying the system so the LM response is clear  
COMM of any influence from the ground. The primary magnetic field coupling 
COMM between the receiver coils and the transmitter loop is continuously   
COMM hardware-monitored, providing a separate value for the magnetic field 
COMM coupling during each transient sounding. These data are used for raw  
COMM data correction in a separate post-processing step. The primary field 
COMM compensation technique has proven stable and has routinely yielded a  
COMM reduction of the primary field influence in very early time gates by  
COMM a factor exceeding 17dB.                                              
COMM
COMM Following PFC on the Low Moment data  the data are normalized in      
COMM respect to the effective Rx coil area, Tx coil area, number of turns  
COMM and current giving the units [pV/(m4*A)].
COMM The EM data is filtered adaptively based on the signal-to-noise ratio 
COMM The applied EM filtering method is based on iterative weighted spline 
COMM fitting routines, which operate in positive/negative symmetric        
COMM transform spaces. The data weighting scheme relies on an extensive    
COMM noise evaluation performed on the individual gate values of the raw   
COMM data decays. Optimised sets of averaging filters are used for each    
COMM measured moment and type of receiver coil in a stepwise averaging     
COMM process. This allows for optimal suppression of motion induced noise  
COMM as well as cultural noise components, while keeping track of the      
COMM resulting data uncertainty.
COMM 
COMM DIGITAL TERRAIN DATA 
COMM The digital terrain was determined by subtracting the processed laser 
COMM altimeter (height above ground) data from the GPS altitude (height    
COMM above the GRS80 ellipsoid) data to yield the height of the ground     
COMM above the GRS80 ellipsoid.  Then the ellipsoid-geoid separation       
COMM (N-value) was subtracted to yield the elevation of the ground above   
COMM the Australian Height Datum (AHD).                                    
COMM
COMM MAGNETIC DATA PROCESSING
COMM Final processing of the magnetic data involved the application of     
COMM conventional corrections to compensate for diurnal variation,         
COMM International Geomagnetic Reference Field (IGRF) removal, and heading 
COMM effects prior to gridding. Processing of magnetic data involved:      
COMM  1 Pre-processing of static (1 Hz) magnetic data acquired at the      
COMM    magnetic base stations
COMM  2 Pre-processing of airborne magnetic data
COMM  3 Standard corrections to compensate the diurnal variation.
COMM  4 IGRF correction
COMM  5 Micro-Levelling of gridded data
COMM
COMM BASE MAG CONSTANTS
COMM For the UDF survey , the base station magnetometers were used at various locations throughout the survey area.
COMM The base station constants, added back into the TMI data after subtraction of the diurnal, were caluclated through 
COMM simultanoues base magnetometer measurements at different locations.  
COMM
